A||||7||Cheever2||Help me choose new hobby: hang gliding or sky diving?||||6||
Z||000000||Cheever2||12-15-2000||07:32 PM||||Hang-gliding lessons = about $120.  I don't know if I could then teach my family or if they each have to pay for lessons too.  Equipment = about $5000 new, maybe $3000 used.  At least that's a 1-time cost, and the family could takes turns with it.<P>Skydiving lessons and equipment = I don't know.  But I do know just paying for a plane to take you up can be expensive (IMO), and it's a recurring cost.<P>Hang-gliding flights last longer.  I would think both would be equally thrilling, which is why I've narrowed it down to a price question.  Anybody here know anything about either of these?<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000001||WampusCat||12-15-2000||08:07 PM||||I've done a little skydiving and it was way cool.  The initial AFF course was $300 and after that it was something like $15 per jump from 12,000'.  I was able to borrow equipment from a friend, not sure what it costs to rent.  A new chute is probably about $2000 and up.  I've never tried hang gliding but I've done lots of soaring in sailplanes and I loved it.  It's pretty expensive unless you find a really good club to join though.  I think hang gliding would be the least expensive since you don't need a tow or ride from an airplane to get up.  What are you going to jump off of with a hang glider in SE TX?  Pretty flat isn't it?  Try searching for some Skydiving or Hanggliding FAQ online to get better info.  BTW, I learned to skydive at a place called sky's the limit in Beeville, TX.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-16-2000||05:43 PM||||Never tried hang gliding, but I took my 1st dive back in '87 (I think).  Not quite as good as sex, but not bad either.  Quite a thrill.  Try 40k with a mask.  It gets cold up there, but you don't really feel it.  I never had to pay for anything since Uncle Sam taught me.  I haven't dove in almost 4 years, though.  Damn, I miss it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||12-17-2000||03:34 PM||||HMMM... Strapped to a large kite or jump out of a perfectly good airplane? I have done neither and I don't wish to try. Basically I would suggest what you are looking for. Falling or flying. If given a choice I'd probably go with hang gliding since it is more like flying and I'd prefer that.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||12-17-2000||03:56 PM||||yeah, I'd go with hang gliding as well.. in fact when I was 12, I tried designing my own hang glider... but since I didn't have much money, and didn't know anything about welding.. I tried to make it out of dowel, from the hobby shop.. LOL..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>luckily I got bored before I finished it, or else I probably would have actually tried it...<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||10
Z||000005||William||12-17-2000||06:14 PM||||Here's a thought. Para planes are a big rage right now for cheap thrills. Those are the three wheelers with propellers that hang below a big nylon wing/parachute. With one type, you can even strap the engine to your back. I think they cost about 5 to 7 grand used and you don't need a mountian! I've always wanted a gyrocopter but realize that it would mean certian death.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever2||12-17-2000||08:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by William:<BR><B>I've always wanted a gyrocopter but realize that it would mean certian death.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>I thought gyrocopters were supposed to be very safe.  The few incidents I've heard of were all homemade jobs.  I got a brochure once from Air Command gyro's and they look like a lot of fun.<P>Thanks for the responses.  I may be doing something else however.  My boys (13 & 15) joined the Civil Air Patrol this year, and are really enjoying it.  CAP's main function is to search for downed aircraft, but they have a lot of activities for kids, to feed their interest in aviation.  I'm not a pilot, but there are still interesting things for adults to do.  I think that's what my new hobby is going to be.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||stsmithgt||12-19-2000||12:42 PM||||I see those hang gliders and para-gliders floating over at the sand hills on the beach catching the updraft.  It looks pretty peacefull.  I suppose for more action, you'd go find a mountain range.  Those guys (and gals) cruise up and down the beach that way.  Too bad it's too cold to get a really good view from up there. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  People are constantly falling out of planes over at the airport in Marina CA..  I watch the go-carts races they have there.  I haven't seen anyone splat yet.  A couple have in the last couple of years though. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> It all looks like fun anyway.||||reg||1
